{"type":"message","id":"fdde75d6","parentId":"9d4d30fb","timestamp":"2026-10-17T22:51:59.427Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py` now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)` instead of `strptime` with a format that had no slot for the trailing `Z`. All 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` now pass, including `test_dates`.\n\nThis works because you're on Python 3.13, where `fromisoformat` accepts `Z` and offsets like `+02:00`. Python 3.10 and earlier reject `Z`.\n\nOne behaviour change: input ending in `Z` or an offset now comes back timezone-aware (UTC for `Z`). Input without one still comes back naive, as before. That matters if a caller compares or subtracts naive and aware datetimes, because Python raises a `TypeError` for that.\n\n`day_key` isn't affected, since it only formats the date part. I didn't look for other callers of `parse_date` outside the test file."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":294,"cacheRead":26066,"cacheWrite":578,"totalTokens":26940,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":578}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792277515606,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011Cg8dSeNozqUoqneJyuEDT","duration":3798.9413590000004,"ttft":3712.7918389999977,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":26646,"nonMessageTokens":21589,"compactionEpoch":0}}}
